\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Quaternion and coordinate selectors}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{\Gamma=Q_8=\{\pm1,\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}} and take
the simple undirected Cayley graph with edges
#{\{g,gs\}}, #{s\in\{\pm i,\pm j\}}, counted once each.
Give each edge weight one, choose any orientation, and set #{m=1}.
The two parts
##{A=\{\pm1,\pm k\},\qquad B=\{\pm i,\pm j\}}
give #{K_{4,4}}: every vertex in one part has the 4 distinct
vertices of the other as its neighbors. In part order,
##{L=\begin{pmatrix}-4I_4&J_4\\J_4&-4I_4\end{pmatrix},
\qquad Q=I_8-L,}
where #{J_4} is the all-ones matrix. The two constant-on-part
vectors give Laplacian eigenvalues #{0,-8}; the two partwise
zero-sum spaces give eigenvalue #{-4}, of total multiplicity 6.}

\p{Left multiplication gives a signed graph action #{\rho} on
#{\mathbb R^{Q_8}}, independent of the arbitrary edge orientations.
For the central involution #{z=-1}, put
##{e_-=(1-[z])/2\in\mathbb R[Q_8],\qquad
P_-=\rho(e_-)=(I-\rho(z))/2.}
Since #{z^2=1} and #{z^{-1}=z}, this is a self-adjoint
idempotent. Its image is the four-dimensional space of fields
#{f(-g)=-f(g)}. The group-algebra decomposition
#{\mathbb R[Q_8]\cong\mathbb R^4\times\mathbb H}
in \ref{fgap-001B} sends #{e_-} to #{(0,1)}: each real character
takes #{z} to #{1}, while the quaternionic realization takes it
to #{-1}. Thus this selector retains precisely the quaternion factor
in the regular module, without assigning it a physical interpretation.}

\proof{
\p{An odd field has zero sum on each of #{A,B}, since each part
is a union of pairs #{\{g,-g\}}. Therefore #{J_4} kills its
restriction to either part, and the displayed Laplacian gives
#{Lf=-4f}. An orthonormal basis is
#{(\delta_g-\delta_{-g})/\sqrt2} for #{g=1,i,j,k}.
Hence the [selected precision](fdip-005B) is
##{S_-=5I_4,\qquad C_-=I_4/5.}
Equivalently, the two neighbor contributions indexed by #{s,-s}
cancel on an odd field. The positive mass supplies the shift from
#{-4} to #{5}.}
}

\p{Compare the coordinate selector on the subgroup
#{R=\langle i\rangle=\{1,-1,i,-i\}}, which retains two vertices
from each part. In the full graph, decompose fields into the two
constant-on-part lines and the two partwise zero-sum spaces. On the
normalized part constants, the precision and covariance are
##{\begin{pmatrix}5&-4\\-4&5\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\frac19\begin{pmatrix}5&4\\4&5\end{pmatrix};}
on the zero-sum spaces the covariance is #{1/5}. It follows that
full covariance entries are #{13/45} on the diagonal,
#{4/45} between distinct vertices in the same part, and #{1/9}
between parts. For example the first entry is
#{(1-1/4)/5+(5/9)/4=13/45}.}

\p{In retained order #{(1,-1,i,-i)}, compression therefore gives
##{C_R=\frac1{45}\begin{pmatrix}
13&4&5&5\\4&13&5&5\\5&5&13&4\\5&5&4&13
\end{pmatrix}.}
The two within-part difference vectors have covariance eigenvalue
#{(13-4)/45=1/5}. On normalized retained part constants the
covariance block is
#{\frac1{45}\begin{pmatrix}17&10\\10&17\end{pmatrix}},
with eigenvalues #{3/5,7/45}. Inversion gives the retained
precision spectrum
##{\operatorname{spec}(S_R)=\{5/3,5,5,45/7\}.}
It differs from #{\{5,5,5,5\}} for the quaternion selector despite
equal rank and the same full background precision.}

\p{The chosen #{Q_8} action is auxiliary data used to construct
#{P_-}; it is not another slot of the [selected tuple](fdip-005A).
Indeed the underlying graph has #{2(4!)^2=1152} automorphisms:
its connected bipartition is unique up to exchange, and every
permutation within either part, as well as their exchange, preserves
all edges. The selected-background groupoid remembers the graph and
the resulting projector, not a named subgroup of this automorphism
group. Remembering the action would require an enhanced tuple and
arrows intertwining that action.}
